  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 230 PM CDT Wed Aug 5 2026\n\nThe primary upper level features remain slow to evolve over the \nCONUS with a broad cyclonic flow regime in place stretching from the \nGreat Lakes back through the northern Rockies. Over the Desert \nSouthwest a weak anticyclone remains with subtropical moist plume \nnoted across northern Mexico into the Baja region. Across the local \narea, a weak modified frontal boundary has very slowly been being \npushed southeast across the area by a weak upper level shortwave \nrounding the base of the broader cyclonic flow. \n\nThis frontal zone remain the focus for much of the next 12 to 18 hrs \nacross the area. A few breaks in the clouds have resulted this \nafternoon and overall the low ceilings have lifted through the day \nwith general warming of the BL and then also slightly drier air \nslowly being advected into the area from Nebraska well behind the \nfront. This should remain the trend into the overnight period. \nHowever, a few strong to possibly a severe storm or two may be \npossible over mainly east-central areas but one or two could form \nalong I-70 this evening into early overnight over central Kansas \nareas once the LLJ becomes active. Overall, forecast soundings do \nhave some steep mid-level lapse rates and the shear within the layer \nof instability could support a few strong storms that persist long \nenough to develop hail that cold be around quarter size. Wind may \nbecome a hazard with early storms but appears to be an overall low \nrisk. Brief heavy rain mainly over east-central areas may cause \nareal flooding if storms are slow moving enough. At this point, not \nexpecting major flooding issues but not zero risk either with PW \nvalues approaching 2 inches at across parts of east-central into far \nnortheast Kansas areas. This overall threat generally lasts from \nthis evening through part of the overnight period before the LLJ \nveers and storms should then focus south and east of the area.  \n\nThursday night and Friday morning could see more chances for showers \nand storms with another weak embedded shortwave working through the \nupper pattern but the base of the broader trough should shift east \nwith the better ascent focused east of the general area. Still, a \nweak lee trough with nocturnal LLJ may support overnight showers and \nembedded storms late Thursday night into early Friday morning.  \n\nThe heat becomes the focus through the weekend into Monday with the \nhottest days trending into Sunday/Monday. Can't rule out later heat \nheadlines being needed. But right now, generally good agreement in \nthe western CONUS ridge spreading east into the area appears on \ntrack. Some ensemble data suggest H85 temps from 25-30C range set up \nacross the area which if no storms end up moving into the area then \nexpect generally clear enough conditions that insolation and dry \nadiabatic mixing will push surface temps to the mid to upper 90s \nwith dewpoints in low to mid 70s again will yield heat index values \naround 103-105F degrees over at least part of the area Sunday and \nMonday. Still too much uncertainty in the overall setup due to the \nnortheastern flank of the broader ridge could be impacted by \nnocturnal storm systems moving through portions of the central \nPlains into the mid and upper MS Valley regions.",
